{
  "run_id": "PMC12566232__scimon__trend_niche_ground_b_o3mini__s2",
  "auditor": "trapi:gpt-5.6-sol_2026-07-09",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The initial HfZrTi HEA is single-phase.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The initial HfZrTi HEA has limited plasticity and toughness.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "A restrictive crystal structure and limited slip systems cause the low plasticity and toughness of HfZrTi.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adding 2–10 at.% Cu to HfZrTi will promote formation of an FCC phase.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Cu-modified HfZrTi alloy will have a dual-phase microstructure consisting of an FCC phase alongside the original phase.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The newly formed FCC phase will be ductile.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Arc melting followed by thermomechanical processing can refine the microstructure of the modified alloy.",
      "field": "intervention",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC12298864::chunk::000::abstract::934a070a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "no"
    },
    {
      "text": "Copper has a strong tendency to stabilize FCC structures in high-entropy alloy systems.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Copper addition is expected to lower the stacking-fault energy of HfZrTi.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Lower stacking-fault energy will enable additional active slip systems.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Greater dislocation mobility can enhance ductility.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC8398136::chunk::000::abstract::92875111a7"
      ],
      "same_system": "no"
    },
    {
      "text": "Copper addition may induce transformation-induced plasticity in HfZrTi.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proposed FCC formation, added slip activity, and enhanced dislocation motion will increase the ductility of HfZrTi.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proposed modification will increase the toughness of HfZrTi.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Copper addition could compromise the high-temperature strength of HfZrTi.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The beneficial effects are specifically expected within the 2–10 at.% Cu range.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cu additions outside the 2–10 at.% range may fail to stabilize the desired FCC phase or may adversely affect high-temperature strength.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "X-ray diffraction and electron microscopy can be used to determine whether Cu addition changes the phase constitution and dual-phase fraction.",
      "field": "falsification_experiment",
      "needs_support": false,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC12298864::chunk::000::abstract::934a070a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "no"
    },
    {
      "text": "Comparison with a 0 at.% Cu control can test whether Cu addition improves tensile ductility and fracture toughness.",
      "field": "falsification_experiment",
      "needs_support": false,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    }
  ],
